#!/bin/sh

__help_text() {
cat << 'EOT'
NAME
  iocell - jail manager amalgamating ZFS, VNET and resource limits

SYNOPSIS
  iocell [-v] activate ZPOOL
  iocell [-v] cap UUID|TAG
  iocell [-v] clean [-f] [-a|-r|-j|-t]
  iocell [-v] clone UUID|TAG[@snapshot] [property=value]
  iocell [-v] console [-f] UUID|TAG
  iocell [-v] create [-e] [release=RELEASE] [pkglist=file]
      [property=value] [count=value]
  iocell [-v] deactivate ZPOOL
  iocell [-v] defaults
  iocell [-v] destroy [-f] UUID|TAG
  iocell [-v] df
  iocell [-v] exec [-u username | -U username] UUID|TAG|ALL command [arg ...]
  iocell [-v] export UUID|TAG
  iocell [-v] fetch [release=RELEASE | ftphost=ftp.hostname.org |
                    ftpdir=/dir/ | ftpfiles="base.txz lib32.txz src.txz"]
                    [ ftplocaldir=/dir/ ] [ compression=ALGO ]
  iocell [-v] get property|all UUID|TAG
  iocell [-v] get -r property|all
  iocell [-v] help
  iocell [-v] import IMAGE_UUID_PREFIX [legacy-property=value ...]
  iocell [-v] init-host IP ZPOOL
  iocell [-v] inuse [UUID|TAG]
  iocell [-v] pkg UUID|TAG pkg_command [arg ...]
  iocell [-v] limits [UUID|TAG]
  iocell [-v] list [-t|-r|-s jid|uuid|boot|state|tag|type|ip4|release]
  iocell [-v] promote UUID|TAG
  iocell [-v] rcboot
  iocell [-v] reboot|restart [-s] UUID|TAG|ALL [UUID|TAG|ALL ...]
  iocell [-v] rcshutdown
  iocell [-v] reset UUID|TAG|ALL
  iocell [-v] rollback UUID|TAG@snapshotname
  iocell [-v] runtime UUID|TAG
  iocell [-v] send [-c] [-m] [-r -h HOST -u USER]
      [-i SNAP1 -i SNAP2 | -I SNAP1 -I SNAP2] POOL
  iocell [-v] set property=value [property=value] UUID|TAG|default
  iocell [-v] snaplist UUID|TAG
  iocell [-v] snapmount UUID|TAG@snapshotname DESTINATION
  iocell [-v] snapremove UUID|TAG@snapshotname|ALL
  iocell [-v] snapshot|snap [-f] [-r] UUID|TAG[@snapshotname]
  iocell [-v] snapumount UUID|TAG@snapshotname
  iocell [-v] start [-f] UUID|TAG|ALL [UUID|TAG|ALL ...]
  iocell [-v] stop UUID|TAG|ALL [UUID|TAG|ALL ...]
  iocell [-v] uncap UUID|TAG
  iocell [-v] update [-n] UUID|TAG
  iocell [-v] upgrade UUID|TAG [release=RELEASE]
  iocell [-v] version | --version

DESCRIPTION
  iocell is a system administration tool for jails designed to simplify
  jail management tasks. It abstracts away the management of ZFS backed jails
  running VNET or shared IP networking with optional support for resource
  limits.

  Both, shared IP based jails and VNET enabled jails are supported.

  Each jail has a unique ID (UUID) automatically generated at creation time.
  Using the UUID as a jail identifier means that a jail can be replicated in a
  distributed environment with greater flexibility. This also eliminates
  potential naming clashes on large scale deployments and helps reduce
  operator error.

  Use full UUIDs or tags for jail operations. Short UUID prefixes are currently
  unreliable. Import separately accepts a unique image UUID prefix.

  A descriptive TAG (hostname, label, etc.) makes jails easier to identify.
  Alias names cannot be empty, contain a slash, or equal "." or "..". Template
  tags have stricter ZFS component rules. An unset tag defaults to a timestamp.
  Retagging requires mounted dataset owners, not merely plausible directories.
  Returned write/link/alias-retirement failures restore configs and aliases
  where possible; incomplete recovery is reported with retained recovery paths.
  Private retired-alias backups are removed after commit. If that final cleanup
  fails, the error explicitly says the retag committed and names its backup.
  This is not a concurrent-writer or power-loss atomicity guarantee.

  Jail configuration is stored in UCL files alongside datasets, with storage
  properties managed by ZFS. uclcmd is a required dependency. Replication
  transfers the selected filesystem tree; basejail images still need their
  shared release files on the receiving host.

  Use a supported FreeBSD release with ZFS, jail support, uclcmd and an existing
  ZFS pool. Additional kernel features are needed only for their corresponding
  optional functions:
    o  VIMAGE/VNET support for vnet=on; shared-IP jails do not require it.
    o  RACCT and RCTL, with kern.racct.enable=1 at boot, for resource limits.

  Check the running kernel's capabilities before enabling those features.

  The -v switch before a command enables verbose shell tracing. Unknown
  commands, missing required operands and malformed option/argument lists
  return nonzero with diagnostics before pool/filesystem setup or dispatch.
  No-argument usage, help and version return success. This is syntax checking,
  not complete property/kernel validation or a transaction across targets;
  a later runtime error can follow earlier successful operations. Some legacy
  execution paths still have misleading statuses; inspect resulting state.
  Compatibility with 2.2.0:
    o  Experimental push is now disabled. Its old implementation was marked
       not working and could stage unrelated files or push the wrong repository
       from the caller's working directory. Use Git directly in an explicitly
       selected worktree; there is no replacement iocell push command.
    o  fetch -p/-P and update -p/-P are now rejected. They depended on obsolete
       portsnap shared ports management. Manage a ports checkout separately
       with Git, or use packages; no new iocell ports backend replaces them.
    o  update -n remains Git-jail-only. Ordinary-image properties that were
       silently ignored are now rejected; use set after import. Legacy tar
       archives still accept creation properties.
    o  create -b/-c/-t and base= were already unsupported in 2.2.0 despite old
       recipes. Historical chroot, record, package and show commands were
       already undispatched. These are documentation corrections, not newly
       removed working features.

  Start, stop, reboot/restart (including -s), rcboot and rcshutdown attempt all
  selected jails and return the first failure reported by synchronous
  operations. Successful operations are not undone if another jail fails.

SUBCOMMANDS

  activate ZPOOL

    Intended for automation tools. The pool can be activated for iocell jails
    without requiring user input.

  cap UUID|TAG

    Reapply resource limits on jail while it is running.

  clean [-f] (-a|-r|-j|-t) [...]

    At least one cleanup mode is required. Modes may be combined; -f applies
    to all selected modes regardless of its position. The -a mode takes
    precedence over other modes and runs once. Invalid syntax is rejected
    before cleanup, and a failed mode stops the remaining modes.

    This removes the ZFS datasets associated with the flag called. The -a flag
    will destroy everything associated with iocell. The -r flag stops all
    jails first and refuses cleanup if any cannot be stopped. It then removes
    downloaded archives and fetched RELEASE datasets. Basejails rely on these
    releases; do not use -r while you still need them. The -j flag destroys
    the jails dataset but preserves aliases of retained templates. It refuses
    dependent clones outside the jails tree even with -f. The -t flag will
    destroy the templates dataset.
    Same warning applies here as with cleaning the RELEASE dataset.
    Template cleanup includes clones inside the templates tree. Without -f,
    it refuses clones outside that tree. With -f, it prints ZFS's destruction
    preview for the templates tree and all dependent trees, then destroys them.
    If -a removes the datasets but cannot remove the leftover host directory,
    it reports both facts and returns an error; it does not erase host files.

  clone UUID|TAG[@snapshot] [property=value]

    Create a new jail identity using a ZFS clone of the selected source.
    Optional property=value operands configure the copy. UUID@snapshot or
    TAG@snapshot selects an existing point in time. Existing delegated data
    requires its matching snapshot too; otherwise cloning is refused. Without
    a snapshot operand, a fresh recursive source snapshot is created.

    Examples:

    Clone the current state of the jail:
    iocell clone UUID tag=www02

    Clone a jail from a custom snapshot (snapshot taken previously):
    iocell clone UUID@snapshotname tag=www02

  console [-f] UUID|TAG

    Execute login to open a shell inside the jail. With -f, start a stopped
    jail first; if startup fails, return the error without attempting login.
    An already running jail is not restarted. A stopped template cannot be
    started through console; convert it back to a jail first.

  create [-e] [release=RELEASE] [pkglist=file] [property=value] [count=value]

    Create a basejail using a fetched shared release. Without release=, the
    host release is selected. Shared base directories are mounted read-only;
    this is not an independent copy of the complete OS. The -e switch creates
    an empty filesystem, not a clone or populated guest userland. Old -b, -c,
    -t and base= were already unsupported in 2.2.0 despite older recipes.
    Use clone for an existing source; no working creation mode was removed.

    Example: iocell create tag=www01 pkglist=$HOME/my-pkgs.txt
             iocell create release=15.1-RELEASE tag=mybasejail vnet=off

    By default only one jail will be created, but if you specify count=NUMBER,
    then that many jails will be created.

    Example: iocell create tag="mybasejail" host_hostname="mybasejail" count=10

    This will create 10 jails with the tag mybasejail_{1-10}. The tag and
    hostname will be incremented up with the number of jails you wish to create.

  deactivate ZPOOL

    Currently prints manual dataset-removal guidance. It does not clear the
    pool discovery comment or disable jails. Do not treat it as deactivation;
    manually destroying data is a separate, irreversible operator decision.

  defaults

    Display defaults stored in the selected pool's UCL configuration.
    Use set property=value default to change defaults for subsequent create
    commands. Explicit creation properties take precedence; existing jails
    and configurations copied by clone/import are not rewritten.

  destroy [-f] UUID|TAG [...]

    Destroy the given jails irreversibly. Without -f, confirmation is required
    and a running jail is refused. With -f, confirmation is bypassed and a
    running jail must stop successfully before destruction. A failed target
    stops the remaining targets and returns an error. Nonempty host-side
    directories left after dataset removal are retained, not recursively
    erased. Verify identity/backups.

  df

    List disk space related information. Available fields:

    CRT - compression ratio
    RES - reserved space
    QTA - disk quota
    USE - used space
    AVA - available space
    TAG - jail name

  exec [-u username | -U username] UUID|TAG|ALL command [arg ...]

    Execute command inside the jail. This is simply an iocell UUID/tag wrapper
    for jexec(8).

  export UUID|TAG

    Export a stopped jail as a recursive raw ZFS stream under /iocell/images.
    Ordinary exports do not necessarily produce a tar.xz archive or SHA256
    sidecar. Basejail shared release files are not part of the jail image.
    Images are private files. Existing image or .partial names are refused,
    never truncated. Failed sends retain the .partial image and export snapshot.
    Only a completed stream is published under the final name. Snapshot-cleanup
    failure returns nonzero even when a complete image has been published.
    Check the image format and required release before restoring it.

  fetch [release=RELEASE | ftphost=ftp.hostname.org |
        ftpdir=/dir/ | ftpfiles="base.txz lib32.txz src.txz"]
        [ ftplocaldir=/dir/ ] [ compression=ALGO ]

    Used for downloading and updating/patching releases.

    Activate a pool first, then select an available release. The interactive
    prompt accepts a release name and defaults to the host release. Supply a
    suitable mirror/path if it no longer provides that distribution. Use trusted
    distribution files and include complete sources for etcupdate preparation.

    Example: iocell fetch release=15.1-RELEASE

    fetch can also used to update already downloaded releases. To update a local
    release already present in iocell (iocell list -r) run:

             iocell fetch release=15.1-RELEASE

    This patches an existing prepared release without re-extracting archives.
    Processing an existing release requires every iocell jail on the host to
    be stopped, including thick jails and jails using other releases or pools.
    Local distribution fetches skip network patching. New basejails use the
    resulting shared files.
    Fetch, extraction, patching and etcupdate failures return nonzero. Partial
    datasets/downloads are retained, never recursively erased on failure.
    An incomplete retained release is refused on retry. Inspect and back up its
    data/cache first. To discard only an unneeded incomplete release, stop all
    iocell jails and verify the exact pool, release, mounts and dependencies:

        zfs list -r jailpool/iocell/releases/15.1-RELEASE
        zfs list -r jailpool/iocell/download/15.1-RELEASE
        zfs destroy -r jailpool/iocell/releases/15.1-RELEASE
        zfs destroy -r jailpool/iocell/download/15.1-RELEASE
        iocell fetch release=15.1-RELEASE

    Substitute your pool/release; omit commands for absent datasets. These
    commands discard partial data/cache; never use them on a needed release.
    Do not add -R or -f to bypass clone or busy-mount refusals, and never erase
    host directories recursively.
    clean -r removes all releases, not just this one.
    The original resolver is restored and newly mounted filesystems are cleaned
    up; an incomplete cleanup is reported without discarding recovery data.

    Obsolete -p/-P ports options are rejected before initialization. Manage
    a modern ports tree separately with Git.

    compression: Compression is lz4 by default; see zfs(8) for the list of
    compression algorithms available.

    ftplocaldir: Specifying ftplocaldir=/path/to/distributions fetches files
    from a local directory. This is useful when a user wants a custom build
    release or use iocell offline.

    Example: iocell fetch release=15.1-RELEASE ftplocaldir=/path/to/dist

  get property|all UUID|TAG
  get -r property|all

    Get named property or if "all" keyword is specified dump all properties
    known to iocell.

    To display whether resource limits are enforced for a jail:

    iocell get rlimits UUID|TAG
    iocell get -r rlimits
    iocell get all UUID|TAG

    Use defaults rather than get all default. Unknown get options and missing
    operands are rejected before property lookup. A stored literal string null
    is distinguished from a missing configuration key.

  help

    This help file you are reading.

  import IMAGE_UUID_PREFIX [legacy-property=value ...]

    Select a trusted compatible image under /iocell/images by full UUID or
    unique literal basename prefix, not a filesystem path. Fetch the required
    release first. Ordinary ZFS streams differ from legacy thick-jail archives
    and do not universally verify SHA256 sidecars. Ordinary imports reject
    extra property operands and never select .partial images; use set after
    restoring. Legacy tar.xz archives still accept creation properties and
    matching SHA256 sidecars. Images are not renamed to guess their format.
    Ordinary ZFS receive failures return nonzero, retain diagnostics and do
    not print success. The image is retained; any received datasets are left
    for inspection, not automatically destroyed. Inspect partial state before
    retrying. Legacy extraction failures are also nonzero and name the retained
    partial jail; legacy archives remain a separate format.
    Verify actual configuration and content before starting a restored jail.

  init-host IP ZPOOL

    Initialize a remote host for iocell. iocell will send all required ZFS
    filesystems over the network to the host. This requires SSH working keys
    installed for root.

  inuse [UUID|TAG]

    Display consumed resources for a jail. Without UUID or TAG dump all
    resources for all running jails in a comma delimited form.

  pkg UUID|TAG pkg_command [arg ...]

    Run pkg for an already running jail selected by tag or full UUID. A quoted
    multi-word argument can currently be split; avoid such wrapper forms or
    use an explicitly controlled command inside the running jail.

  limits [UUID|TAG]

    Display active resource limits for a jail or all jails. With no UUID
    supplied display all limits active for all jails. An empty result can
    currently return a nonzero status; inspect actual kernel rules.

  list [-t|-r|-s FIELD]

    List jails, templates (-t), fetched releases (-r), or sort the jail table
    (-s) by jid, uuid, boot, state, tag, type, ip4 or release. Non-iocell jails
    are listed only when running. Nested backup/delegated datasets are not
    separate identities and cannot regenerate an existing parent config.

  promote UUID|TAG

    Reverse the native ZFS clone/source relationship. This does not turn
    shared basejail release mounts into private copies. See zfs(8).

  rcboot

    Start all jails with "boot" property set to "on". Intended for boot time
    execution. Jails will be started in an ordered fashion based on their
    "priority" property. Priority 99 jails start in the background; their
    failures are reported but do not affect the exit status. Their output is
    retained in the iocell root's log/UUID-rcboot.log. If the shared log
    directory cannot be prepared, rcboot returns a setup error before starting
    any jail.

  reboot [-s] UUID|TAG|ALL [UUID|TAG|ALL ...]

    Fully stops the jail and, upon success, starts it again. With -s, runs the
    configured exec_stop command, signals remaining processes, then runs
    exec_start while retaining the kernel jail and networking. Soft restart
    does not guarantee graceful shutdown or recovery of every application.

  rcshutdown

    Stop all jails with "boot" property set to "on". Intended for full host
    shutdown.
    Jails will be stopped in an ordered fashion based on their "priority"
    property.

  reset UUID|TAG|ALL

    Resets properties using built-in values while carrying forward selected
    identity/network settings, including TAG, UUID and generated VNET MACs.
    Reset does not reload defaults saved by set ... default; those values
    apply to later creation. Use ALL to target all jails.

  restart [-s] UUID|TAG|ALL [UUID|TAG|ALL ...]

    Alias for reboot: default is hard stop/start. Specify -s for a soft restart
    retaining the kernel jail and networking. It does not guarantee application
    consistency; use the application's shutdown/recovery procedure.

  rollback UUID|TAG@snapshotname

    Stop the jail before rolling back to an existing snapshot. A normal
    snapshot restores only the jail's root dataset. A recursive snapshot
    restores the jail dataset and its descendants. Every target must have
    the requested snapshot; otherwise no rollback is performed.
    Rollback does not restore native ZFS properties or tag symlinks. A restored
    tag property may therefore differ from the current alias namespace.

    WARNING: Changes and newer snapshots on the affected datasets will be
    lost. Shared basejail release datasets are not restored. Multi-dataset
    rollback is not atomic: a ZFS error stops further rollbacks, but earlier
    datasets may already have been restored. For more information read zfs(8).

  runtime UUID|TAG

    Show runtime configuration of a jail. Useful for debugging.

  send [-c] [-m] [-r -h HOST -u USER]
       [-i SNAP1 -i SNAP2 | -I SNAP1 -I SNAP2] POOL

    Takes a recursive snapshot of the active SOURCEPOOL/iocell dataset and
    sends it to POOL/iocell. Supports remote, incremental and move operations.
    Every full send creates a unique fresh snapshot; snapshot or pipeline
    failures are returned as errors. A failed full transfer reports and retains
    its source snapshot for recovery. Existing snapshot-bearing replicas may
    require incremental streams rather than another full receive.
    Moving (-m) removes the original source only after successful transfer,
    destination activation and mounting. It is only available locally.
    A failed activation retains source data but may have relocated its
    mountpoint; inspect state and explicitly reactivate it before use.
    Remote (-r) requires a user (-u), host (-h), and publickey
    authentication. If using incremental (-i) or Incremental (-I) be sure to
    supply the flag for each snapshot.

    Clean (-c) removes older reserved ioc-send-* snapshots below iocell only
    after a successful transfer, keeping the new common replication point.
    Failed transfers retain earlier bases. Other snapshots and dependent
    clones are not destroyed to make cleanup succeed; failures are returned.
    Local transfers clean both ends. Remote -c cleans only the sender;
    manage receiver retention separately. Do not combine clean with
    incrementals.
    Remote destinations are validated on the receiver, not locally.
    Identical names on different hosts are valid, not self-send.

    Local Example: iocell send newpool
    Incremental Example: iocell send -i old/iocell@s1 -i old/iocell@s2 new
    Remote Example: iocell send -r -u root -h some.host newpool

    The received pool is not mounted if received locally, but is mounted if
    received remotely.

  set property=value [property=value] UUID|TAG|default

    Set one or more per-jail properties, or persist creation defaults in the
    selected pool's .default UCL file. Example: iocell set vnet=off default.
    Defaults apply to subsequent create commands, with explicit properties
    taking precedence. Existing jails are unchanged.

    Pool selection, generated identity/runtime fields, template selection or
    conversion, and read-only ZFS properties are not creation defaults:
    pool, tag, host_hostname, host_hostuuid, hostid, last_started,
    jail_zfs_dataset, template, istemplate, origin, mountpoint, compressratio,
    available and used. Set applicable fields explicitly per jail; select
    pools with activate. Other native defaults, such as compression, are
    stored in UCL and applied to newly created jail datasets, not their parent.

    Unknown names, empty values and invalid on/off switches are rejected.
    Names and some values are validated, not every kernel combination.
    Defaults are replaced only after a successful UCL write. Multiple settings
    are applied individually, stopping on the first failure; this is not a
    batch transaction. Reset-to-saved-defaults behavior is not restored here.
    Quote values for your invoking shell. Spaces, apostrophes and equals
    signs within a property value are preserved literally.

    Per-jail configuration writes retain config.old as a last-write backup.
    Later writes overwrite that one file rather than accumulating backups.
    Tag recovery may consume it. This is not a backup of the jail or of an
    entire multi-property command.

  snaplist UUID|TAG

    List all snapshots belonging to jail.

        NAME    - snapshot name
        CREATED - creation time
        RSIZE   - referenced size
        USED    - used space

  snapmount UUID|TAG@snapshotname DESTINATION

    This will mount the specified jail's snapshot on the location given.

  snapremove UUID|TAG@snapshotname|ALL

    Destroy specified jail snapshot. If the keyword ALL is specified all
    snapshots will be destroyed for the jail.

  snapshot|snap [-f] [-r] UUID|TAG[@snapshotname]

    Create a ZFS snapshot for jail. If the -r flag is specified it will by
    recursively snapshot POOL/iocell/jails/UUID. Otherwise it will only snapshot
    POOL/iocell/jails/UUID/root. If no snapshot name is specified defaults
    to auto snapshot name based on current date and time.

    The -f flag is to bypass the interactive question if you have a running
    database in the jail. Make sure to pass -f on it's own and first before any
    other flag like -r.

    Example: iocell snapshot -f -r JAIL

  snapumount UUID|TAG@snapshotname

    Will unmount the specified jail's snapshot.

  start [-f] UUID|TAG|ALL [UUID|TAG|ALL ...]

    Start jail identified by UUID or TAG. If the -f switch is supplied, it will
    force start a template for editing. This flag does not apply to normal
    jails.

  stop UUID|TAG|ALL [UUID|TAG|ALL ...]

    Stop jail identified by UUID or TAG. If ALL is specified, it will stop all
    jails.

  uncap UUID|TAG

    Release resource limits on the fly. Successful removal and an empty rule
    set return zero; kernel removal/query failures return nonzero.

  update [-n] UUID|TAG

    Patch the selected userland with freebsd-update where supported. Basejail
    updates affect the shared release used by other jails too. Non-basejail
    updates take a jail-root back-out snapshot; it does not back up a shared
    release. Use a maintenance window and separate release/application backups.
    Obsolete -p/-P shared ports paths are rejected before initialization.
    The -n option checks a Git jail's remote status without merging.

  upgrade UUID|TAG [release=RELEASE]

    By default this will upgrade jail RELEASE to match the host's RELEASE
    unless another RELEASE is specified with the "release" property.

    Please note: The upgrade process for basejails is done by setting the jail's
    "release" property to the required new RELEASE and then merging /etc.
    If you wish to do this yourself without having /etc merged do:

    iocell set release=15.1-RELEASE UUID|TAG

    For this the RELEASE must be locally available. The basejail
    will re-clone its filesystems from the new release at next jail start.

    Fetch the target release and stop the jail first. Ordinary basejail upgrades
    require complete release sources and no preexisting mounts below the jail
    root. Template-based basejails must use a target-release template instead;
    their in-place upgrade is refused. Build, merge and unresolved-conflict
    failures return nonzero and restore the old config/root where possible.
    The /etc backup, root snapshot and single config.old backup are retained;
    inspect them before removing stale upgrade recovery points and retrying.
    An incomplete backout is reported without deleting those recovery points.
    Mail queue creation and newaliases run after a successful merge; failures
    follow the same backout path. Inspect warnings and test the result; no
    zero-downtime guarantee is made. Full independent-userland upgrades may need
    interactive confirmations and multiple install passes. Old create -c/-t
    recipes are not supported.

  version | --version

    Print the iocell version. Include the installed package version or
    checkout commit when reporting problems from a modified source tree.

PROPERTIES
  For more information on properties please check the relevant man page which
  is noted under each property in the form of "Source: manpage". Source "local"
  marks iocell specific properties.

  pkglist=none | path-to-file

    A readable text file containing one package name per line. Blank lines
    and lines beginning with # are ignored. Packages are installed during
    jail creation. Relative paths are resolved from the calling directory.
    Works only in combination with the create subcommand.

    Creation stops at the first package or mount error. The failed jail is
    retained for inspection, is not started, and is not reported as successfully
    created. Further jails requested with count are not created. Packages
    already installed are not rolled back. Installation mounts are cleaned up;
    a cleanup failure also makes creation fail.

    Default: none
    Source: local

  vnet=on | off

    This controls whether to start the jail with VNET or a shared IP
    configuration. Default is to auto-guess from a sysctl. If you don't
    need a fully virtualized per jail network stack set it to off.

    Default: auto-guess
    Source: local

  bpf=on | off

    This controls whether to start the jail with BPF devices enabled.
    Default is off.

    Default: off
    Source: local

  dhcp=on | off

    This controls whether to start the jail with DHCP.
    Default is off, but if you want it on, vnet and bpf are required.

    Default: off
    Source: local

  ip4_addr="interface|ip-address/netmask"

    The IPv4 address for VNET and shared IP jails.

    Form is: interface|ip-address/netmask
    Multiple interfaces:
    "interface|ip-address/netmask,interface|ip-address/netmask"

    For shared IP jails if an interface is given before
    the IP address, an alias for the address will be added to that
    interface, as it is with the interface parameter. If the DEFAULT
    tag is used instead of an interface, the interface will be
    automatically assigned based upon the system's default interface.

    If a netmask in either dotted-quad or CIDR form is given after IP
    address, it will be used when adding the IP alias.

    If the AUTOIP4 tag is used in place of an ip-address, the IP address
    will be automatically assigned at first start of the jail. This
    requires that the ip4_autostart and ip4_autoend variables are set on
    the 'default' property source. If used, the IP4 address will be set
    to the first available based upon the given range and already created
    jails.

    These ranges can be stored with set property=value default. Check the
    selected address and interface before exposing services. Explicit addresses
    or a configured DHCP setup are alternatives to automatic range allocation.

    For VNET jails the interface will be configured with the IP addresses
    listed.

    Example: "vnet0|192.168.0.10/24,vnet1|10.1.1.10/24"
    This would configure interfaces vnet0 and vnet1 in a VNET jail. In this
    case no network configuration is necessary in the jail's rc.conf file.

    Default: none
    Source: jail(8)

  ip4_saddrsel=1 | 0

    Only takes effect when vnet=off.
    A boolean option to change the formerly mentioned behaviour and
    disable IPv4 source address selection for the prison in favour of
    the primary IPv4 address of the jail.  Source address selection
    is enabled by default for all jails and the ip4_nosaddrsel
    settting of a parent jail is not inherited for any child jails.

    Default: 1
    Source: jail(8)

  ip4=new | disable | inherit

    Only takes effect when vnet=off.
    Control the availability of IPv4 addresses.  Possible values are
    "inherit" to allow unrestricted access to all system addresses,
    "new" to restrict addresses via ip4.addr above, and "disable" to
    stop the jail from using IPv4 entirely.  Setting the ip4.addr
    parameter implies a value of "new".

    Default: new
    Source: jail(8)

  createbridge=on | off

    Controls whether default bridge0 and bridge1 will be created
    on jails's start.

    Default: on

  keepbridge=on | off

    Controls whether bridges bridge0 and bridge1 will be destroyed
    on jail's shutdown.

    Default: off

  defaultrouter=none | ipaddress

    Setting this property to anything other than none will try to configure a
    default route inside a VNET jail.

  defaultrouter6=none | ip6address

    Setting this property to anything other than none will try to configure a
    default IPv6 route inside a VNET jail.

  resolver=none | nameserver IP;nameserver IP;search domain.local

    Sets the jail's resolver (resolv.conf). Fields must be ; delimited
    which are translated to newlines in resolv.conf.

    If the resolver is set to none (default) the jail will inherit
    the hosts resolv.conf file.

  ip6.addr, ip6.saddrsel, ip6

    A set of IPv6 options for the prison, the counterparts to
    ip4.addr, ip4.saddrsel and ip4 above.

  interfaces=vnet0:bridge0,vnet1:bridge1 | vnet0:bridge0

    By default there are two interfaces specified with their bridge
    association. Up to four interfaces are supported. Interface configurations
    are separated by commas. Format is interface:bridge, where left value is
    the virtual VNET interface name, right value is the bridge name where the
    virtual interface should be attached.

    Default: vnet0:bridge0,vnet1:bridge1
    Source: local

  host_domainname="NIS domainname"

    The NIS domainname of the jail.

    Default: none
    Source: jail(8)

  host_hostname=UUID

    The hostname of the jail.

    Default: UUID
    Source: jail(8)

  exec_fib=0 | 1 ..

    The FIB (routing table) to set when running commands inside the jail.

    Default: 0
    Source: jail(8)

  devfs_ruleset=4 | 0 ..

    The number of the devfs ruleset that is enforced for mounting
    devfs in this jail.  A value of zero (default) means no ruleset
    is enforced.  Descendant jails inherit the parent jail's devfs
    ruleset enforcement.  Mounting devfs inside a jail is possible
    only if the allow_mount and allow_mount_devfs permissions are
    effective and enforce_statfs is set to a value lower than 2.
    Devfs rules and rulesets cannot be viewed or modified from inside
    a jail.

    NOTE: It is important that only appropriate device nodes in devfs
    be exposed to a jail; access to disk devices in the jail may permit
    processes in the jail to bypass the jail sandboxing by modifying
    files outside of the jail.  See devfs(8) for information on
    how to use devfs rules to limit access to entries in the per-jail
    devfs.  A simple devfs ruleset for jails is available as ruleset
    #4 in /etc/defaults/devfs.rules

    Default: 4
    Source: jail(8)

  mount_devfs=1 | 0

    Mount a devfs(5) filesystem on the chrooted /dev directory, and
    apply the ruleset in the devfs_ruleset parameter (or a default of
    ruleset 4: devfsrules_jail) to restrict the devices visible
    inside the jail.

    Default: 1
    Source: jail(8)

  exec_start="/bin/sh /etc/rc"

    Command(s) to run in the prison environment when a jail is created.
    A typical command to run is "sh /etc/rc".

    Default: /bin/sh /etc/rc
    Source: jail(8)

  exec_stop="/bin/sh /etc/rc.shutdown"

    Command(s) to run in the prison environment before a jail is
    removed, and after any exec_prestop commands have completed.
    A typical command to run is "sh /etc/rc.shutdown".

    Default: /bin/sh /etc/rc.shutdown
    Source: jail(8)

  exec_prestart="/usr/bin/true"

    Command(s) to run in the system environment before a jail is started.

    Default: /usr/bin/true
    Source: jail(8)

  exec_prestop="/usr/bin/true"

    Command(s) to run in the system environment before a jail is stopped.

    Default: /usr/bin/true
    Source: jail(8)

  exec_poststop="/usr/bin/true"

    Command(s) to run in the system environment after a jail is stopped.

    Default: /usr/bin/true
    Source: jail(8)

  exec_poststart="/usr/bin/true"

    Command(s) to run in the system environment after a jail is started,
    and after any exec_start commands have completed.

    Default: /usr/bin/true
    Source: jail(8)

  exec_clean=1 | 0

    Run commands in a clean environment.  The environment is discarded
    except for HOME, SHELL, TERM and USER.  HOME and SHELL are
    set to the target login's default values.  USER is set to the
    target login.  TERM is imported from the current environment.
    The environment variables from the login class capability database
    for the target login are also set.

    Default: 1
    Source: jail(8)

  exec_timeout=60 | 30 ..

    The maximum amount of time to wait for a command to complete.  If
    a command is still running after this many seconds have passed,
    the jail will be terminated.

    Default: 60
    Source: jail(8)

  stop_timeout=30 | 60 ..

    The maximum amount of time to wait for a jail's processes to
    exit after sending them a SIGTERM signal (which happens after the
    exec_stop commands have completed).  After this many seconds have
    passed, the jail will be removed, which will kill any remaining
    processes. If this is set to zero, no SIGTERM is sent and the
    prison is immediately removed.

    Default: 30
    Source: jail(8)

  exec_jail_user=root

    The user to run commands as, when running in the jail environment.

    Default: root
    Source:  jail(8)

  exec_system_jail_user=0 | 1

    This boolean option looks for the exec_jail_user in the system
    passwd(5) file, instead of in the jail's file.

    Default: 0
    Source: jail(8)

  exec_system_user=root

    The user to run commands as, when running in the system environment.
    The default is to run the commands as the current user.

    Default: root
    Source: jail(8)

  mount_fdescfs=1 | 0

    Mount a fdescfs(5) filesystem in the jail's /dev/fd directory.
    Note: This is not supported on FreeBSD 9.3.

    Default: 1
    Source: jail(8)

  mount_procfs=0 | 1

    Mount a procfs(5) filesystem in the jail's /dev/proc directory.

    Default: 0
    Source: local

  mount_linprocfs=0 | 1

    Mount a linprocfs(5) filesystem in the jail's /compat/linux/proc directory.

    Default: 0
    Source: local

  enforce_statfs=2 | 1 | 0

    This determines which information processes in a jail are able to
    get about mount points.  It affects the behaviour of the following
    syscalls: statfs(2), fstatfs(2), getfsstat(2) and fhstatfs(2)
    (as well as similar compatibility syscalls).  When set to 0, all
    mount points are available without any restrictions.  When set to 1,
    only mount points below the jail's chroot directory are visible
    In addition to that, the path to the jail's chroot directory
    is removed from the front of their pathnames.  When set to 2
    (default), above syscalls can operate only on a mount-point where
    the jail's chroot directory is located.

    Default: 2. jail(8)

  children_max=0 | ..

    The number of child jails allowed to be created by this jail (or
    by other jails under this jail). This limit is zero by default,
    indicating the jail is not allowed to create child jails.  See
    the Hierarchical Jails section for more information in jail(8).

    Default: 0
    Source: jail(8)

  login_flags="-f root"

    Supply these flags to login when logging in to jails with the console
    function.

    Default: -f root
    Source: login(1)

  jail_zfs=on | off

    Enables automatic ZFS jailing inside the jail. Assigned ZFS dataset will
    be fully controlled by the jail.
    NOTE: Setting this to "on" automatically enables allow_mount=1
    enforce_statfs=1 and allow_mount_zfs=1! These are dependent options
    required for ZFS management inside a jail.

    Default: off
    Source: local

  jail_zfs_dataset="zfs_filesystem ..."

    This is a space-separated list of datasets to be jailed and fully handed
    over to a jail.
    Takes a ZFS filesystem name; if it begins with "iocell/jails", it will be
    created on the ZFS pool that iocell uses.
    NOTE: only valid if jail_zfs=on. By default the mountpoint is set to none,
    to mount this dataset set its mountpoint inside the jail i.e. "zfs set
    mountpoint=/data full-dataset-name" and issue "mount -a".

    Default: iocell/jails/UUID/root/data
    Source: local

  jail_zfs_mountpoint=/path/in/jail

    If only a single ZFS filesystem is assigned to a jail via the
    jail_zfs_dataset setting, this property may be used to manipulate its
    mountpoint within the jail.
    The default value of "none" allows the mountpoint to be managed
    from within the jail by setting the ZFS "mountpoint" property on the
    dataset directly.
    When multiple ZFS filesystesm are assigned to a jail, this property
    must be "none", and the filesystem mountpoints are manipulated directly
    using the ZFS "mountpoint" properties for the assigned datasets.

    Default: none
    Source: local

  securelevel=3 | 2 | 1 | 0 | -1

    The value of the jail's kern.securelevel sysctl.  A jail never
    has a lower securelevel than the default system, but by setting
    this parameter it may have a higher one.  If the system
    securelevel is changed, any jail securelevels will be at least as
    secure.

    Default: 2
    Source: jail(8)

  allow_set_hostname=1 | 0

    The jail's hostname may be changed via hostname(1) or sethostname(3).

    Default: 1
    Source: jail(8)

  allow_sysvipc=0 | 1

    A process within the jail has access to System V IPC
    primitives.  Prior to FreeBSD 11.0, System V primitives
    share a single namespace across the host and jail
    environments, meaning that processes within a jail would be
    able to communicate with (and potentially interfere with)
    processes outside of the jail, and in other jails.  In
    FreeBSD 11.0 and later, this setting is deprecated in favor
    of sysvmsg, sysvsem, and sysvshm.

    Default: 0
    Source: jail(8)

  sysvmsg=disable | inherit | new

    Allow access to SYSV IPC message primitives.  If set to
    inherit, all IPC objects on the system are visible to this
    jail, whether they were created by the jail itself, the base
    system, or other jails.  If set to new, the jail will have
    its own key namespace, and can only see the objects that it
    has created; the system (or parent jail) has access to the
    jail's objects, but not to its keys.  If set to disable, the
    jail cannot perform any sysvmsg-related system calls.
    Ignored in FreeBSD 10.3 and earlier.

    Default: new
    Source: jail(8)

  sysvsem=disable | inherit | new

    Allow access to SYSV IPC semaphore primitives in the same
    manner as sysvmsg.  Ignored in FreeBSD 10.3 and earlier.

    Default: new
    Source: jail(8)

  sysvshm=disable | inherit | new

    Allow access to SYSV IPC shared memory primitives in the
    same manner as sysvmsg.  Ignored in FreeBSD 10.3 and
    earlier.

    Default: new
    Source: jail(8)

  allow_raw_sockets=0 | 1

    The prison root is allowed to create raw sockets.  Setting
    this parameter allows utilities like ping(8) and
    traceroute(8) to operate inside the prison.  If this is
    set, the source IP addresses are enforced to comply with
    the IP address bound to the jail, regardless of whether
    or not the IP_HDRINCL flag has been set on the socket.
    Since raw sockets can be used to configure and interact
    with various network subsystems, extra caution should be
    used where privileged access to jails is given out to
    untrusted parties.

    Default: 0
    Source: jail(8)

  allow_chflags=0 | 1

    Normally, privileged users inside a jail are treated as
    unprivileged by chflags(2).  When this parameter is set,
    such users are treated as privileged, and may manipulate
    system file flags subject to the usual constraints on
    kern.securelevel.

    Default: 0
    Source: jail(8)

  allow_mount=0 | 1

    privileged users inside the jail will be able to mount
    and unmount file system types marked as jail-friendly.
    The lsvfs(1) command can be used to find file system
    types available for mount from within a jail.  This permission
    is effective only if enforce_statfs is set to a
    value lower than 2.

    Default: 0
    Source: jail(8)

  allow_mount_devfs=0 | 1

    privileged users inside the jail will be able to mount
    and unmount the devfs file system.  This permission is
    effective only together with allow.mount and if
    enforce_statfs is set to a value lower than 2.  Please
    consider restricting the devfs ruleset with the
    devfs_ruleset option.

    Default: 0
    Source: jail(8)

  allow_mount_nullfs=0 | 1

    privileged users inside the jail will be able to mount
    and unmount the nullfs file system. This permission is
    effective only together with allow_mount and if
    enforce_statfs is set to a value lower than 2.

    Default: 0
    Source: jail(8)

  allow_mount_procfs=0 | 1

    privileged users inside the jail will be able to mount
    and unmount the procfs file system.  This permission is
    effective only together with allow.mount and if
    enforce_statfs is set to a value lower than 2.

    Default: 0
    Source: jail(8)

  allow_mount_tmpfs=0 | 1

    privileged users inside the jail will be able to mount
    and unmount the tmpfs file system.  This permission is
    effective only together with allow.mount and if
    enforce_statfs is set to a value lower than 2.
    Note: This is not supported on FreeBSD 9.3.

    Default: 0
    Source: jail(8)

  allow_mount_zfs=0 | 1

    privileged users inside the jail will be able to mount
    and unmount the ZFS file system.  This permission is
    effective only together with allow.mount and if
    enforce_statfs is set to a value lower than 2.  See
    zfs(8) for information on how to configure the ZFS
    filesystem to operate from within a jail.

    Default: 0
    Source: jail(8)

  allow_quotas=0 | 1

    The jail root may administer quotas on the jail's
    filesystem(s). This includes filesystems that the jail
    may share with other jails or with non-jailed parts of
    the system.

    Default: 0
    Source: jail(8)

  allow_socket_af=0 | 1

    Sockets within a jail are normally restricted to IPv4,
    IPv6, local (UNIX), and route.  This allows access to
    other protocol stacks that have not had jail functionality
    added to them.

    Default: 0
    Source: jail(8)

  host_hostuuid=UUID

    Default: UUID
    Source: jail(8)

  tag="any string"

    Custom string for aliasing jails. Must be unique.

    Default: date@time
    Source: local

  istemplate=yes | no

    This property controls whether the jail is a template. Templates are not
    started automatically. Set to yes to convert a stopped basejail into
    a template. Set to no to convert it back, preserving its stored UUID,
    tag, snapshots and child datasets.

    Stop the template first. Invalid stored UUIDs and dependent root clones
    prevent conversion back. A failed ZFS rename leaves the template flags
    unchanged and is reported as an error. Conversion in either direction
    preserves the previous jailed state of a legacy data child. A template
    tag must start with a letter, digit or underscore and contain only letters,
    digits, underscores, periods, colons or hyphens. Retag generated timestamp
    names before conversion, e.g. set tag=mytemplate UUID. Invalid tags are
    rejected before rename, metadata or alias changes. (See EXAMPLES section)

    Default: no
    Source: local

  boot=on | off

    If set to "on" jail will be auto-started at boot time (rcboot subcommand)
    and stopped at shutdown time (rcshutdown subcommand). Jails will be started
    and stopped based on their priority value.

    Default: off
    Source: local

  notes="any string"

    Custom notes for miscellaneous tagging.

    Default: none
    Source: local

  owner=root

    The owner of the jail, can be any string.

    Default: root
    Source: local

  priority=99:0
    Start priority at boot time, smaller value means higher priority.
    Also, for shutdown the order will be reversed.

    Default: 99. Max: 99. Lowest: 0.

    Anything set to 99 will automatically start in the background parallel
    with other jails set to 99. Anything less than 99 will start sequentially.

  last_started

    Last successful start time. Auto set every time jail starts.

    Default: timestamp
    Source: local

  type=basejail

    Currently only basejails, gitjails and empty jails are supported.

    Default: basejail
    Source: local

  hostid=UUID

    The UUID of the host node. Jails won't start if this property differs from
    the actual UUID of the host node. This is to safeguard jails from being
    started on different nodes in case they are periodically replicated across.

    Default: UUID of the host (taken from /etc/hostid)
    Source: local

  release=RELEASE

    Select a compatible fetched release, for example 15.1-RELEASE. Changing
    this value alone does not fetch the release or merge /etc for an upgrade.

    Default: the host's RELEASE
    Source: local

  compression=on | off | lz4 | zstd | gzip | gzip-N | zle | lzjb

    Select a compression algorithm supported by the host's ZFS version and
    pool features. The meaning of on depends on ZFS defaults/features; do not
    assume it always means lzjb. Modern OpenZFS also provides zstd levels and
    fast variants; consult zfsprops(7) and zpool-features(7) on the actual host.
    The native shortened property name is compress. Changes affect newly
    written data, not existing blocks.

    Default: lz4
    Source: zfs(8)

  origin

    This is only set for clones. Read-only.
    For cloned file systems or volumes, the snapshot from which the clone
    was created. See also the clones property.

    Default: -
    Source: zfs(8)

  quota=15G | 50G | ..

    Quota for jail.
    Limits the amount of space a dataset and its descendents can consume.
    This property enforces a hard limit on the amount of space used. This
    includes all space consumed by descendents, including file systems
    and snapshots. Setting a quota on a descendent of a dataset that
    already has a quota does not override the ancestor's quota, but
    rather imposes an additional limit.

    Default: none
    Source: zfs(8)

  mountpoint

    Path for the jail's root filesystem. Don't tweak this or jail won't start!

    Default: set to jail's root
    Source: zfs(8)

  compressratio

    Compression ratio. Read-only.
    For non-snapshots, the compression ratio achieved for the used space
    of this dataset, expressed as a multiplier.  The used property
    includes descendant datasets, and, for clones, does not include the
    space shared with the origin snapshot.

    Source: zfs(8)

  available

    Available space in jail's dataset.
    The amount of space available to the dataset and all its children,
    assuming that there is no other activity in the pool. Because space
    is shared within a pool, availability can be limited by any number of
    factors, including physical pool size, quotas, reservations, or other
    datasets within the pool.

    Source: zfs(8)

  used

    Used space by jail. Read-only.
    The amount of space consumed by this dataset and all its descendents.
    This is the value that is checked against this dataset's quota and
    reservation. The space used does not include this dataset's reservation,
    but does take into account the reservations of any descendent
    datasets. The amount of space that a dataset consumes from its parent,
    as well as the amount of space that are freed if this dataset is
    recursively destroyed, is the greater of its space used and its
    reservation.

    When snapshots (see the "Snapshots" section) are created, their space
    is initially shared between the snapshot and the file system, and
    possibly with previous snapshots. As the file system changes, space
    that was previously shared becomes unique to the snapshot, and
    counted in the snapshot's space used. Additionally, deleting snapshots
    can increase the amount of space unique to (and used by) other
    snapshots.

    The amount of space used, available, or referenced does not take into
    account pending changes. Pending changes are generally accounted for
    within a few seconds. Committing a change to a disk using fsync(2) or
    O_SYNC does not necessarily guarantee that the space usage information
    is updated immediately.

    Source:  zfs(8)

  dedup=on | off | verify | sha256[,verify]

    Deduplication for jail.
    Configures deduplication for a dataset. The default value is off.
    The default deduplication checksum is sha256 (this may change in the
    future).  When dedup is enabled, the checksum defined here overrides
    the checksum property. Setting the value to verify has the same
    effect as the setting sha256,verify.

    If set to verify, ZFS will do a byte-to-byte comparsion in case of
    two blocks having the same signature to make sure the block contents
    are identical.

    Default: off.
    Source: zfs(8)

  reservation=size | none

    Reserved space for jail.
    The minimum amount of space guaranteed to a dataset and its descendents.
    When the amount of space used is below this value, the dataset
    is treated as if it were taking up the amount of space specified by
    its reservation. Reservations are accounted for in the parent
    datasets' space used, and count against the parent datasets' quotas
    and reservations.

    Default: none
    Source: zfs(8)

  sync_target

    This is for future use, currently not supported.

  sync_tgt_zpool

    This is for future use, currently not supported.

  rlimits=on | off

    If set to "on" resource limits will be enforced.

    Default: off
    Source: local

  cpuset=1 | 1,2,3,4 | 1-2 | off

    Controls the jail's CPU affinity. For more details please refer to
    cpuset(1).

    Default: off
    Source: cpuset(1)

RESOURCE LIMITS
  Resource limits (except cpuset and rlimits) use the following value
  field formatting in the property: limit:action.

  Limit defines how much of the resource a process can use before the
  defined action triggers.

  Action defines what will happen when a process exceeds the allowed
  amount.

  Valid actions are:
        deny    deny the allocation; not supported for cpu and
                wallclock
        log     log a warning to the console
        devctl  send notification to devd(8)
        sig*    e.g. sigterm; send a signal to the offending
                process

  To better understand what this means please read rctl(8)
  before enabling any limits.

  The following resource limits are supported:

  memoryuse=limit:action | off

    Limits the resident set size (DRAM).

    Default: 8G:log
    Source: rctl(8)

  memorylocked=limit:action | off

    Limits locked memory.

    Default: off
    Source: rctl(8)

  vmemoryuse=limit:action | off

    Virtual memory limit (swap + DRAM combined)

    Default: off
    Source: rctl(8)

  maxproc=limit:action | off

    Limit maximum number of processes.

    Default: off
    Source: rctl(8)

  cputime=limit:action | off

    Limit CPU time, in seconds.

    Default: off
    Source: rctl(8)

  pcpu=limit:action | off

    Limit %CPU, in percents of a single CPU core or hardware thread.

    Default: off
    Source: rctl(8)

  datasize=limit:action | off

    Limit data size.

    Default: off
    Source: rctl(8)

  stacksize=limit:action | off

    Limit stack size.

    Default: off
    Source: rctl(8)

  coredumpsize=limit:action | off

    Limit core dump size.

    Default: off
    Source: rctl(8)

  openfiles=limit:action | off

    Limit file descriptor table size (number of open files).

    Default: off
    Source: rctl(8)

  pseudoterminals=limit:action | off

    Limit number of PTYs.

    Default: off
    Source: rctl(8)

  swapuse=limit:action | off

    Limit swap usage.

    Default: off
    Source: rctl(8)

  nthr=limit:action | off

    Limit number of threads.

    Default: off
    Source: rctl(8)

  msgqqueued=limit:action | off

    Limit number of queued SysV messages.

    Default: off
    Source: rctl(8)

  msgqsize=limit:action | off

    Limit SysV message queue size.

    Default: off
    Source: rctl(8)

  nmsgq=limit:action | off

    Limit number of SysV message queues.

    Default: off
    Source: rctl(8)

  nsemop=limit:action | off

    Limit number of SysV semaphores modified in a single semop(2) call.

    Default: off
    Source: rctl(8)

  nshm=limit:action | off

    Limit number of SysV shared memory segments.

    Default: off
    Source: rctl(8)

  shmsize=limit:action | off

    Limit SysV shared memory size.

    Default: off
    Source: rctl(8)

  wallclock=limit:action | off

    Limit wallclock time.

    Default: off
    Source: rctl(8)

EXAMPLES
  Set up iocell from scratch:

    iocell activate ZPOOL
    iocell fetch release=15.1-RELEASE

  Create first jail:

    iocell create release=15.1-RELEASE tag=myjail vnet=off

  List jails:

    iocell list

  Start jail:

    iocell start UUID

  Turn on resource limits and apply them:

    iocell set rlimits=on UUID
    iocell cap UUID

  Display resource usage:

    iocell inuse UUID

  Convert jail into template:

    iocell set istemplate=yes UUID

  List templates:

    iocell list -t

  Clone jail from template:

    iocell clone TAG-of-template tag=myjail

  Import a compatible trusted image on another host

    iocell import UUID

  Get the last successful start time for all jails

    iocell get -r last_started

HINTS
  The active pool is recorded in UCL defaults; legacy discovery can also use
  a pool comment. Do not assume deactivate clears either selection mechanism.

  VNET requires an appropriate bridge, routing and firewall policy. A physical
  bridge member can expose jail MACs upstream. If a provider prohibits extra
  MACs, use a private bridge with host routing/NAT instead; iocell does not
  automatically configure that NAT. Preserve management access while testing.

  Per-jail firewall support depends on the host kernel, permissions and device
  exposure; verify these capabilities on the host.
  Do not blindly disable bridge filtering or IPv6 duplicate-address detection.

  Validate actual properties and kernel behavior for your host release.
  Remaining command defects are documented in the repository's known issues.

  The actual jail name in the jls(8) output is set to ioc-UUID. This is a
  required workaround as jails will refuse to start with jail(8) when name
  starts with a "0".

  To prevent dmesg leak inside jails apply the following sysctl:

    security.bsd.unprivileged_read_msgbuf=0

  Enable forwarding only when the host is intentionally routing jail traffic,
  and configure bridge filtering and NAT for your security boundary. There is
  no universally safe set of pfil sysctls to copy into every host.

  For more information please visit:

    https://github.com/iocell/iocell

